// Demo blocks.
//
// Written against #185. Every claim below was checked against the crate
// (`crates/wcl_wdoc/src/demo.rs`, the dispatch in `render/html.rs`,
// `markdown/emit.rs` and `pdf/collect.rs`, and `crates/wcl_wdoc/lib/demo.wcl`)
// and against real builds of the snippets — including the two live `demo`
// blocks on this page, which are the chapter demonstrating itself.
page wdoc_demo {
  title = "Demo blocks"

  chapter_header "Demo blocks" {
    kicker       = "wdoc · reference"
    reading_time = "9 min read"
    updated      = "2026-08-20"
    version      = "wcl 0.33.2-alpha"
  }

  p "Documentation about a block has to answer two questions at once: what do I write, and what do I get? Answer them separately and you write the component twice, once in a `code` block and once for real. The two copies drift the moment you edit one. A `demo` removes the second copy. You author the component once, as the demo's children. wdoc renders those children live and prints their source beside them, so the listing cannot disagree with the picture."

  p "wdoc renders them twice over: once under the site's light palette and once under its dark one, side by side. A reader sees both without touching the theme toggle."

  p "Every file and every command below was run before it was written down, and the two demos on this page are the block demonstrating itself. Type them and compare."

  h2 "A demo in one file"

  p "Save this as `demo.wcl`. It is a whole document rather than a fragment, with a site, a page and one `demo`:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "demo.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
import <wdoc.wcl>

site probe {
  title = "Demo probe"
  theme = :nord
  toc { chapter "Probe" { page = p1 } }
}

page p1 {
  title = "Probe"

  h1 "Probe"

  demo {
    title = "A tip, both ways"
    callout "Tip" {
      class = ["tip"]
      body  = "Author once, see both."
    }
  }
}
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl wdoc build demo.wcl --out out
wrote 1 page
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The same block, live. Everything below the caption is what `demo.wcl` produces:"

  demo {
    title = "A tip, both ways"
    callout "Tip" {
      kind = :tip
      body  = "Author once, see both."
    }
  }

  h2 "What lands on the page"

  p "A demo emits three things, in this order:"

  list {
    li "**The caption.** The `title` field, if you set one, as a small uppercase label."
    li "**The preview.** A two-column row holding the children rendered twice: `<div class=\"wdoc-body wdoc-theme-light wdoc-preview\">` on the left, `wdoc-theme-dark` on the right, under a `Preview` label."
    li "**The example.** The children's formatted source in a `<pre class=\"code-block\"><code class=\"language-wcl\">`, highlighted exactly as a [`code wcl` block](wdoc_code) would be, under an `Example` label."
  }

  callout "Preview first, then example" {
    kind = :note
    body  = "The rendered panes come **above** the listing in HTML. The Markdown backend puts them the other way round, source fence first and render after. No field on the block controls the order. Each backend chose its own. If your prose says \"the code below\", it is wrong in one of the two outputs."
  }

  p "An empty `demo { }` is legal, and it is not the same as writing nothing. You still get the `Preview` label and two empty panes. The `Example` section is the one part that disappears when there is no source to print."

  h2 "The fields"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Field"    | "Type"        | "Default" | "What it does" |
      | "`title`"  | "`utf8?`"     | "unset"   | "A caption above the preview. Rendered in HTML only." |
      | "`diagram`"| "`bool`"      | "`false`" | "Centre and fit the preview contents. See [Diagram previews](#diagram-previews)." |
      | "`id`"     | "`identifier?`" | "unset" | "An explicit `id` on the wrapper `<div>`, so a link can address the demo." |
      | "children" | "`list<ContentBlock>`" | "empty" | "The components to show. Any content block is legal, and a demo can hold several." |
  }

  p "There is no limit of one child. A demo holding three `list` blocks prints all three as one listing and renders all three in each pane. That is how you compare variants of a block side by side, in one demo rather than three."

  h3 "Write title as a field, not as a label"

  p "`title` is declared `@inline(0)`, so the label form parses and `wcl check` accepts it. Rewrite the demo at the top of this chapter as `demo \"A tip, both ways\" { … }` and nothing complains:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check demo.wcl
OK
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The caption is gone from the page all the same. The renderer looks `title` up **by name**, and a positional label is not a readable field. [Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents) states that rule, and the missing caption is what the rule costs you here. Nothing warns you. Write `title = \"A tip, both ways\"` inside the braces."

  h2 "The two panes"

  p "Neither pane is a screenshot and neither is an iframe. Both are ordinary HTML in the same page, and what makes them differ is one CSS rule per palette."

  p "A themed site emits its `--wdoc-*` custom properties four ways: at `:root` for the default mode, under `prefers-color-scheme`, under the `data-theme` attribute the toggle sets, and on two bare classes, `.wdoc-theme-light` and `.wdoc-theme-dark`. The last of the four is the one a demo needs. Custom properties inherit, and the closest ancestor that defines one wins. `.wdoc-theme-light` on a wrapper therefore re-themes everything inside that wrapper, whatever the reader has set globally. That is the whole mechanism. [Themes](wdoc_styling) covers where those properties come from."

  p "Two things follow. A demo shows both palettes **at once** rather than one and then the other, so the reader compares instead of remembering. And a document with no `site` block has no scoped palette rules to emit, so both panes fall back to the neutral `.wdoc-preview` defaults and look identical. The demo still works. It just has nothing to contrast."

  h2 "How many times the children render"

  p "Once per pane. wdoc renders the children twice rather than rendering them once and copying the result."

  p "Rendering twice is the more expensive of the two options, and it is deliberate. HTML content would re-theme for free from one render: the two wrappers redefine the custom properties, and the same markup picks up different colours in each. SVG content does not. A wireframe widget bakes its resolved palette into `fill` and `stroke` attributes in Rust rather than deferring to `currentColor`, because the PDF backend has no CSS to defer to. One render of a wireframe therefore carries one palette, and dropping that string into both panes would put dark-mode greys inside the light pane."

  p "So the demo renderer flips the build's UI theme mode, renders the children, flips it back, and renders them again. To watch it happen, put a wireframe on a page, inside a `diagram`, because a `wf_*` widget is a diagram shape and not a page block. Then read the two panes' `fill` attributes. They differ."

  p "The cost of a second pass is the renderer's side effects, not the markup. Images, icons and videos register themselves as they render, and those registries are keyed by source, so the second pass adds nothing. A demo containing one `callout \"Tip\"` renders two lightbulb icons and the site's `_wdoc/icons.svg` sprite sheet still holds exactly one `lucide-lightbulb` symbol."

  callout "Read the panes as siblings, not as a before and after" {
    kind = :info
    body  = "The two passes see the same document, the same children and the same base directory. Only the UI theme mode differs. A block whose output depends on anything else, such as a counter, a random value, or an ordering that accumulates across a page, would render two different things into the two panes. The demo would then be showing you a bug rather than a palette."
  }

  h2 "Diagram previews"

  p "A prose block fills whatever width you give it. A diagram is the size its canvas says it is, and a half-page pane is rarely that size. A narrow flowchart sits in the top-left corner of a mostly empty box, twice over. `diagram = true` fixes that."

  demo {
    title = "diagram = true"
    diagram = true
    diagram {
      width  = 100
      height = 220
      layout = :layered
      process "parse" { id = parse }
      process "check" { id = check }
      process "build" { id = build }
      parse -> check
      check -> build
    }
  }

  p "The flag changes the CSS class on the preview row, and nothing else. `wdoc-preview-diagram` makes each pane a centring flex container and caps its diagram viewport and any `svg` at `max-width: 100%`, so a compact drawing sits in the middle of its pane and a wide one shrinks to fit instead of scrolling."

  callout "diagram = true does not stack the panes" {
    kind = :warning
    body  = "The row stays a two-column grid either way, and the flag only centres and fits the contents. The viewport is what stacks the panes. Below `48rem` the grid collapses to one column for every demo, flagged or not. To see a wide diagram at full size, that narrow-viewport layout is the only thing that gives it to you."
  }

  p "Set the flag for anything in the diagram family: flowcharts, sequence and state diagrams, charts, timelines, tilemaps, and wireframes. Leave it off for prose, tables, lists, callouts and code. Only a few of the diagram family are page blocks in their own right. A shape or a `wf_*` widget goes inside a `diagram`, and the `diagram` is the demo's child. `demo { wf_button \"Save\" }` fails with `block kind 'wf_button' is not allowed inside 'demo'`. See [The diagram canvas](wdoc_diagrams) and [Wireframes](wdoc_wireframe)."

  h2 "What the example listing shows"

  p "The listing is not the bytes you typed. The formatter pretty-prints each child block, and wdoc joins the results with no blank line between them, so what a reader copies is the canonical form of what you wrote. Three consequences follow:"

  list {
    li "**Comments survive.** A `#` or `//` comment inside a child is part of the tree, and it is printed. Use one to annotate the example."
    li "**The formatter normalises.** `//` comes out as `#`, the formatter redoes your alignment, reflows a one-line block onto several, and respells any value it spells differently. The listing under [Diagram previews](#diagram-previews) is the proof: each `process` takes one line in this page's source and prints on three."
    li "**Only blocks are printed.** The demo's own `title`, `diagram` and `id` fields are the demo's, not the example's, and never appear in the listing."
  }

  code wcl {
    filename = "annotated.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
demo {
  title = "Two list styles"
  list {
    // the default is a bullet list
    li "Plain item"
  }

  list {
    style = :numbered   // opt in to numbering
    li "First step"
  }
}
WCL
  }

  p "The listing that reaches the page holds both `list` blocks, both comments rewritten to `#`, the trailing comment re-aligned, and no sign of the `title`. The blank line you left between the two blocks does not survive:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
list {
  # the default is a bullet list
  li "Plain item"
}
list {
  style = :numbered  # opt in to numbering
  li "First step"
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "What each output target shows"

  p "A `demo` is `@native` on all three targets, and every one of them implements it. You never have to hide a demo from a target with `@except`. What the three show is not the same. The panes need CSS custom properties, which only HTML has, and only HTML emits the caption and the highlighted listing. Each static target decided separately how much of the rest to keep."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Target"       | "Caption" | "Preview"                        | "Example listing" |
      | "HTML"         | "Yes"     | "Both palettes, side by side"    | "Yes, highlighted, below the preview" |
      | "Markdown"     | "No"      | "One render, unthemed"           | "Yes, a `wcl` fence above the render" |
      | "PDF"          | "No"      | "One render, in place"           | "**No**, the source is dropped" |
  }

  p "Markdown keeps the comparison a demo exists to make, in the one order Markdown can carry it. Run `wcl wdoc build demo.wcl --out md --type markdown` on the file from the top of this chapter and you get:"

  code markdown {
    filename = "md/p1.md"
    source = <<'MD'
# Probe

```wcl
callout "Tip" {
  class = ["tip"]
  body = "Author once, see both."
}
```

> [!TIP]
> **Tip**
>
> Author once, see both.
MD
  }

  callout "A demo is invisible in a PDF" {
    kind = :warning
    body  = "The PDF backend collects the demo's children in place and returns: no caption, no listing, no wrapper. The children appear as though you had written them directly on the page, and a reader of the PDF has no way to tell a demo was there. If the source listing is the point of a passage, write a separate [`code`](wdoc_code) block for the PDF and hide the demo from it. [Visibility](wdoc_visibility) covers the `backends` axis. [Output targets](wdoc_outputs) covers what each target can do."
  }

  h2 "Three ways to show a block"

  p "A demo is one of three ways, and the other two are still right sometimes. What separates them is what each one costs and what it can prove."

  table {
    rows:
      | "You write"                | "Reader sees"        | "Can they disagree?" | "Reach for it when" |
      | "A `code wcl` block"       | "Source only"        | "Yes, nothing renders it" | "The snippet is a fragment, an error case, or a file that does not belong on this page" |
      | "The block itself"         | "Rendered output only" | "n/a"              | "The block *is* the content, a real callout or a real diagram in your document" |
      | "A `demo`"                 | "Both, from one authoring" | "**No**, one source of truth" | "You are documenting the block, and the reader must copy the source and trust the picture" |
  }

  p "The middle row is the one people reach for by accident. Write the block plainly on a documentation page and the reader gets a result they cannot reproduce, because you never showed them the input. Put it in a `code` block instead and they get an input you never ran. A demo is the only one of the three where the two halves are the same text."

  p "The cost is real and it is short: two render passes, a wider footprint on the page, and nothing at all in a PDF. For a page whose subject *is* a block, that is a good trade. For a page that merely uses one, it is not."

  h2 "Where to go next"

  list {
    li "[Themes](wdoc_styling). The `--wdoc-*` custom properties, the light and dark palettes, and the toggle the demo's panes ignore."
    li "[Code](wdoc_code). The `code` block the example listing is highlighted like."
    li "[Output targets](wdoc_outputs). What HTML, Markdown and PDF can each carry."
    li "[Visibility](wdoc_visibility). `@only` and `@except`, for hiding a demo from a target or a site."
    li "[Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending). How to declare a block of your own, which is the thing you will most want a demo for."
  }
}
